{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low-bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices across most horizons (often by ~2–5 EUR, occasionally more). Apply an upward level shift to the whole path.",
    "Trend/acceleration underreaction: when history transitions from a dip to a rally, the model lags the upswing and fails to capture the sustained climb. Add a positive trend correction that increases with recent positive momentum (e.g., last 5–10 day slope).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: underprediction tends to grow with forecast horizon (errors widen in weeks 2–4). Use a bias term that ramps up with lead time (small in days 1–5, larger by days 15–30).",
    "Over-mean-reversion after strength: forecasts repeatedly drift back toward mid-60s even when the true path stays elevated (upper-60s/near-70). Reduce mean-reversion strength when recent volatility is accompanied by upward drift (i.e., in rising regimes).",
    "Ceiling/level anchoring: model appears anchored to a narrow band (~63–66) and misses regime shifts to higher trading ranges. Introduce regime detection (e.g., if last price breaks above recent 20–30d average by >~1–2 EUR, shift forecast band upward)."
  ]
}
